{
  "dom_id": "step-q115622",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/944d7dfa7895e5eecc2d11065e268fceba725c8181ffa8283d1fcac39d2d5a98.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Given that $f(0)=0,$ which of the following could be the graph of $y=f(x)?$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there are two critical points: \n\n$\\,$ $x=1\\,$ and $\\,x=3.5$\n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,3.5)$ | $3.5$ | $(3.5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical points. Consider the vertical lines $x=1$ and $x=3.5.$ \n\n- Since we are told that $f(0)=0,$ we can draw that point on the graph.\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,1),$ the point at $x=1$ must be lower than the point at $x=0.$\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(1,3.5),$ the point at $x=3.5$ must be higher than the point at $x=1.$\n\nSo, we draw it as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/4e6fcbb39c9750bb41a2b505325a1b11a633ca749d740aea7c6737bee7db7f7a.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,1)$ and increasing $↗$ on $(1,3.5)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left and to the right of $x=1.$\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(1,3.5)$ and decreasing $↘$ on $(3.5,\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes down to the left and to the right of $x=3.5.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/c010f5bb4dcee1241211beefa90e9496045f2b5e5ddc861bd703e8d0cf0f3276.png)\n\n- Finally, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/493dd437fa00fe3d3056dcb6c2a0b291db27d8bdf96e96ee5366d696bf9433e2.png)",
